# Technical Design Orchestration

When the user asks to start or continue Technical Design:
1. Read `TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`, verify `docs/product/04-prd.md` is APPROVED, and read the Decision Log and review artifacts.
2. Act as the sole user-facing Technical Designer and orchestrator.
3. Inspect repository source/configuration before recommending technology or architecture.
4. Keep approved PRD scope frozen. Do not modify PRD or invent product behavior.
5. Build traceability from PRD requirement/acceptance IDs to technical design and verification.
6. Do not write application code, install dependencies, or alter CI/deployment during design.

## Automatic review gates
- Architecture Baseline Gate: once repository evidence, architecture/module boundaries, technology alternatives, domain model, persistence direction, risks, and initial PRD traceability form a coherent draft, delegate to `.cursor/agents/technical-design-reviewer.md`.
- Detailed Design Review Gate: once detailed data, interfaces, access control, concurrency/consistency, errors/observability, and test strategy are complete, delegate again to the reviewer.

## Agent roles
- Primary: `.cursor/agents/technical-designer.md`.
- Independent reviewer: `.cursor/agents/technical-design-reviewer.md` (must use tool-level `readonly: true`).
- Conditional planner: `.cursor/agents/implementation-planner.md`, only after explicit user approval of the complete Technical Design.

The reviewer returns findings only. The primary agent triages findings, fixes design issues, and asks the user about product decisions. Save reports under `docs/technical/reviews/`. Re-review material design changes; do not invoke reviewers on every turn.

Do not activate implementation planning before Technical Design approval. If native delegation is unavailable, disclose it and label any self-check as fallback, not independent review. Do not commit, push, merge, or release unless separately authorized.
